Class Methods as Alternative Constructors
In Python, class methods are often used to define alternative constructors. This means you can create different ways of instantiating objects beyond the usual __init__ method.

Key Points
•	A constructor is typically the __init__ method, which initializes an object.
•	An alternative constructor is a @classmethod that returns an instance of the class, often by processing input data in a different format.
•	The @classmethod decorator ensures the method receives the class (cls) as its first argument, not the instance (self).
 
Example 1: From String
class Person:
    def __init__(self, name, age):
        self.name = name
        self.age = age
    
    @classmethod
    def from_string(cls, data_str):
        name, age = data_str.split(",")
        return cls(name, int(age))

# Usage
p1 = Person("Alice", 30)              # normal constructor
p2 = Person.from_string("Bob,25")     # alternative constructor


Example 2: From Dictionary
class Book:
    def __init__(self, title, author):
        self.title = title
        self.author = author
    
    @classmethod
    def from_dict(cls, data):
        return cls(data["title"], data["author"])

# Usage
book_data = {"title": "1984", "author": "George Orwell"}
b1 = Book.from_dict(book_data)

Benefits
•	Flexibility: Multiple ways to create objects.
•	Readability: Code becomes more expressive (Person.from_string("Bob,25") is clearer than parsing manually).
•	Encapsulation: Keeps parsing/validation logic inside the class..
Class methods in Python are methods bound to the class rather than its instances, and they take cls (the class itself) as their first argument instead of self.
They’re often used for alternative constructors or operations that logically belong to the class.

What Are Class Methods?
•	Defined using the @classmethod decorator.
•	Receive the class (cls) as the first parameter.
•	Can be called on the class itself or on its instances.
•	Useful when you want to create methods that affect the class as a whole, not just individual objects.
Syntax
class MyClass:
    @classmethod
    def my_class_method(cls, arg1, arg2):
        # cls refers to the class
        return f"Called with {arg1} and {arg2}"
Usage:
print(MyClass.my_class_method("Hello", "World"))
obj = MyClass()
print(obj.my_class_method("Hi", "There"))

Example: Alternative Constructor
class Person:
    def __init__(self, name, age):
        self.name = name
        self.age = age
    
    @classmethod
    def from_string(cls, data_str):
        name, age = data_str.split(",")
        return cls(name, int(age))

# Normal instantiation
p1 = Person("Alice", 30)

# Alternative constructor
p2 = Person.from_string("Bob,25")
Here, from_string is a class method that provides a different way to construct a Person object.
When to Use Class Methods
•	Alternative constructors: e.g., datetime.fromisoformat().
•	Factory methods: creating instances with pre-defined configurations.
•	Class-level operations: when logic applies to the class rather than a single instance.
•	Inheritance-friendly design: since cls refers to the actual subclass, class methods adapt naturally.

Difference from Other Methods
Method Type	First Argument	Bound To	Typical Use
Instance Method	self	Object	Access/modify instance data
Class Method	cls	Class	Alternative constructors, class-wide logic
Static Method	None	Neither	Utility functions inside class namespace

In short: use @classmethod when you need methods that work with the class itself, not just its instances. 
They’re especially powerful for creating flexible constructors and factory patterns.

